# {{FLEET_NAME}} — Canon Conventions

> The shared **canonical-data layer** of this fleet: the published business facts humans
> and agents rely on, plus the protocols that define how agents work together. This repo
> is the company's source of truth — versioned, reviewed, co-edited by humans and agents.
> Seeded by `/add-canon` on {{DATE}}; this file is live fleet configuration — edit via PR.

## Layout

```
CONVENTIONS.md      # this file — the rules of the layer
CODEOWNERS          # folder → responsible human counterpart (review routing)
agents/<name>/      # one folder per agent — that agent's published record, owned
protocols/          # inter-agent contracts — schemas, channels, cadences
lint/ tools/ .github/   # deterministic linting, once /add-canon-lint has seeded it
```

## Ownership

- Every `agents/<name>/` folder has exactly **one owner** — the agent named `<name>`.
- The owner commits **directly** inside its own folder and carries the duty to keep it
  true (its `/canon-reconcile` pass verifies and stamps on a schedule).
- **Everyone else — agent or human — changes someone's folder or `protocols/` via
  branch + PR.** CODEOWNERS routes the review. No exceptions for trivial edits.
- **Sanctioned installer writes (creation/scaffolding only, never content):**
  *enrollment seeding* — when an agent joins (`/add-canon`, incl. fleet enrollment), the
  installer may create its `agents/<name>/` stub + CODEOWNERS line; *lint migration
  seeding* — `/add-canon-lint` may scaffold `facts: []` and port front-matter envelopes
  in pre-schema folders. Everything after belongs to the owner.
- Git history is the audit trail: no force-pushes, no history rewrites, ever.

## What belongs here (the publish test)

The structure below says *how* to publish; this section says *what's worth publishing*.
The test, for every candidate fact or doc: **would another agent — or a human — decide
something differently knowing this?** If yes, publish it. If it only describes you, it
belongs in your workspace, not here.

Four kinds of content earn their place:

1. **Stewarded domain facts** — every agent is the fleet's sensor for some slice of
   reality (pipeline counts, pricing floors, client health, routing rules). Publish the
   *reading*, not the sensor's configuration.
2. **Settled positions, with the why** — decisions taken, options rejected, exclusions
   and their rationale, each with provenance. This is what stops re-litigation and what
   a new agent inherits on day one. Keep conviction honest: an active exploration is
   `draft`; a settled position is `canonical`.
3. **Protocols** — every *recurring* cross-agent interaction (how to request research,
   how a handoff escalates) deserves a contract file under `protocols/`. Highest
   leverage per byte in this repo.
4. **Open needs** — what you need and cannot produce yourself ("need primary-source
   data on X"), published as `docs/open-needs.md` and linked from your profile, so
   other agents can fill gaps unprompted instead of waiting to be asked.
5. **Relationship memory** — one doc per counterpart you actually collaborate with
   (`docs/relations/<counterpart>.md`): the working agreements, a capped log of the
   events that shaped the collaboration, the threads still open. What turns an
   incoming ask from a stranger's message into a continuation. See § Relations.

Two anti-patterns account for most low-value canon:

- **Self-describing registry data** — your version, your schedule count, your internal
  cadences. Nobody decides differently knowing them, and they rot within weeks. (Apply
  the test honestly: an escalation channel or a routing rule *does* pass it.)
- **Process state** — active runs, pending requests, this-week focus. That is
  coordination traffic for messages and events; in a durable repo it is stale residue
  within hours.

## Lintable structure

> The two-zone schema below is the convention every folder follows. It becomes
> **mechanically enforced** once `/add-canon-lint` seeds `tools/canon-lint/` + CI
> (severities in `lint/rules.yaml`): the linter proves internal consistency on every
> push; whether a fact still matches reality is each agent's scheduled
> `/canon-reconcile` — the LLM residual the linter deliberately leaves out.

### Folder schema — every `agents/<name>/`

```
agents/<name>/
  profile.md      REQUIRED — identity + index; the reachability root; always status: canonical
  facts.yaml      REQUIRED — the purely lintable zone: the claims other agents may rely on
  docs/           prose zone — explanations and models; front-matter envelope linted, body never read
  files/          shared artifacts (exports, specs, decks) — every file must be referenced somewhere
  NEEDS-REVIEW.md reconcile's flag file — exempt from the schema
```

**The two-zone rule:** if the fleet may depend on it, it is an entry in `facts.yaml`; the doc is
the explanation behind it. Prose stays free — the linter never reads past a doc's front matter.

### `facts.yaml` — the purely lintable zone

```yaml
facts:
  - key: operating-model.review-cadence   # lowercase dotted subject.relation — the conflict grain
    value: "6 weeks — next review 2026-09-01"
    status: canonical                     # canonical | draft | superseded
    updated: 2026-07-23                   # last content change (YYYY-MM-DD)
    review_by: 2026-09-01                 # verification due date — past due on canonical = stale
    source: docs/operating-model.md       # local doc/file (must exist), URL, or an external ref
```

An empty zone is `facts: []`. A `key` has exactly **one home across all folders** (superseded
entries excepted) — two folders claiming the same key is the conflict that kills silent drift.

### Doc envelope — `profile.md` and every `docs/*.md`

```yaml
---
owner: <agent-name>      # must equal the enclosing agents/<name>/ folder
status: canonical        # canonical | draft | superseded
updated: YYYY-MM-DD
review_by: YYYY-MM-DD    # verification due date; canonical + past due = stale
tldr: "One line — what this doc establishes"
---
```

**Statuses separate conviction levels** (ideas must not dress as canon):
- `canonical` — the fleet may rely on it; fully linted; must be linked from `profile.md`.
- `draft` — sandbox-in-place; legal anywhere in the folder, exempt from staleness, and **must
  not** be linked from `profile.md`.
- `superseded` — kept for history; exempt from staleness and the one-home rule.

### Restricted grammar (why these files stay boring)

The lintable zone is a deliberate YAML subset so a zero-dependency linter can parse it exactly:
flat `key: value` scalars only, full-line comments only, values containing `": "` must be quoted.
Everything remains valid YAML for ordinary tools. Richer structure belongs in prose or `files/`.

**Staleness and secrets:** consumers flag anything `canonical` past its `review_by:` — stale
canon is served with a warning, never as silently-current fact. Publish **facts, not working
state**. **No secrets** — credentials, tokens, and keys never land in this repo.

## Protocols

A protocol is a contract two or more agents (or an agent and a human process) agree on:
message shape, channel, cadence, escalation. One file per protocol under `protocols/`,
same doc envelope (owner = the proposing agent), changes via PR with every party's
owner as reviewer.

## Relations — collaboration memory

Protocols say how agents *agreed* to work together; relations record how the work
*actually went*. Each agent keeps **one doc per counterpart** it collaborates with —
`docs/relations/<counterpart>.md` in its **own** folder (standard doc envelope,
`status: canonical`, linked from `profile.md` under a `## Relations` heading) —
created on the first real interaction, never for counterparts it merely knows exist.

Body structure (Recent events is the required core):

```markdown
## Working agreements
<durable hows — channels, expectations; point at protocols/ rather than restating them>

## Recent events            <!-- newest first, capped: keep the last 10 -->
- 2026-08-05 · ← ask · dev requested X (ref) · resolved 08-06: shipped as Y

Earlier: <one-paragraph rolling summary — events trimmed from the cap fold in here>

## Open threads             <!-- asks/commitments awaiting either side; move to events when resolved -->
- 2026-08-01 · → asked dev for Z — awaiting reply
```

The rules that make it work:

- **Read before acting, append before closing.** An incoming message from a counterpart
  is handled *in the context of* its relation doc (plus the counterpart's view of you —
  `/canon-consume <counterpart> relations` serves both sides); the interaction's outcome
  is appended before the turn ends. The log is a side effect of the interaction, never
  a separate chore — separate chores rot.
- **Events, not ticks.** Record what changed the relationship — asks, commitments,
  handoffs, deliveries, breaches, decisions — one line each, with refs. Tick-by-tick
  status is still process state (§ anti-patterns above) and stays out; the log points
  at facts and protocols rather than restating them.
- **Capped, always loadable.** Last 10 events + the rolling `Earlier:` summary; trim on
  every publish. An ever-growing transcript defeats the point.
- **Each side keeps its own view.** A's `relations/b.md` and B's `relations/a.md` are
  independent records under own-folder writes — no shared file, no write conflicts.
  **Divergence between the pair is signal, not error:** an ask A logged that B never
  did is exactly where a dropped thread lives.

## Humans

Humans co-edit under the same rules: any folder via PR; direct pushes only where you are
the listed CODEOWNERS counterpart and the owning agent is informed (its next reconcile
will re-verify what changed).
